{-
   The bit operations and what a condition means - `lib/Core/Bits.yana`'s `Bitwise`, `Integral`
   and `Truth`, cut down to the three classes and nothing else.

   A file of its own for the reason the real one is: `Core` is a directory, and this stage finds
   every file in it exactly as it finds the real library's. The rest of `Bits.yana` - `Bits`,
   `Bounded`, `Signed`, `Magnitude`, the counting instructions - is the library's business and
   arrives with the declarations it needs.

   `Bitwise` deliberately does not hold `&&`, `||` and `!`: those are questions about truth rather
   than about bits, they answer `Bool` rather than the operand's type, and they are plain functions
   over `Truth` in `Core.yana`. The members here are `Bool` and every integer width - the instances
   the compiler generates, `compiler/intrinsic/provided.def` - and any type a program declares one
   for.
-}
@builtin(bitwise)
pub class Bitwise(a):
  fn and(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn or(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn xor(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn not(value: a) -> a

@builtin(integral)
pub class (Num(a), Bitwise(a)) Integral(a):
  fn %(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn shl(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn shr(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn sar(lhs: a, rhs: a) -> a
  fn rol(value: a, count: a) -> a
  fn ror(value: a, count: a) -> a

{-
   What a condition means. `if x`, `if:` cases and `while x` ask this class rather than requiring
   a Bool, so `if items:` and `if i - 1: continue` say what they look like they say. The rule that
   keeps it from being JavaScript's truthiness is that it never applies through a conversion: the
   instance is selected for the condition's own type, so what `if x` means depends on x's type
   alone and not on which Widen instances happen to be in scope. Bool's instance is the identity.

   `@builtin(truth)` is what fastens a condition to it: the compiler asks `truthy` of every tested
   value, and `Bool`'s generated instance expands to nothing, so `if a > b` produces the IR it
   always did.
-}
@builtin(truth)
pub class Truth(a):
  fn truthy(value: a) -> Bool

{-
   Which end of a word the target puts the low byte at, and the constant that answers it -
   `lib/Core/Bits.yana`'s `Endian` and `Target.byteOrder`, cut down to the two declarations.

   The numbering is pinned because the compiler agrees with it from the other side: `builtinValue`
   answers this role with these numbers, so it is a fact the declaration is depended on for rather
   than one it happens to have.

   **The compiler supplies the constant** - `@builtin(byteOrder)`, and the `Little` below is what the
   declaration is required to say rather than what it holds. Its value is not one the resolver has:
   one resolution serves every target, so a byte order is no more answerable here than how wide a
   `Size` is. What a program gets is an ordinary immutable global with a constant initializer, which
   folds into its readers - so `Target.byteOrder == Big` costs nothing and the arm it guards is gone
   before any machine sees it.
-}
pub data Endian = @value(0) Little | @value(1) Big

@builtin(byteOrder)
pub let Target.byteOrder = Little :: Endian

{-
   Counting the bits of an integer - `lib/Core/Bits.yana`'s `Bits`, cut down to the class.

   Five members, and **the order is the interface**: the compiler reaches each by position, and
   `known.def`'s `bits` row is the second place that order is written down. The instances are the
   compiler's - `provided.def`, at the widths a machine counts in, which is 32, 64 and the target's
   word and is not every integer: a narrow value is some bits of a wider register, so a count of a
   `U8`'s storage and a count of its value are different numbers.

   **All three counts are total, and the answer at zero is stated rather than left to the machine.**
   `countBits(0)` is 0, and both zero counts answer the type's own width. x86's bare `bsf` and `bsr`
   leave their destination unwritten at zero, which is not a value this language can name, so the
   alternative to a defined answer is undefined behaviour reachable from safe source.

   `bitsUpTo` keeps the low `count` bits and clears the rest - x86's `bzhi`. The count is read as an
   unsigned number and **saturates at the type's width**, so a caller that computed a count and did
   not bound it gets the answer the bound would have given.

   `bitWidth` is how many bits it takes to write the value - 0 for zero, 32 for a `U32` with its top
   bit set. A member rather than a function beside the class because it is the one of the five that
   has to name the type's own width, which a body generic in `a` has no way to write down.
-}
@builtin(bits)
pub class (Integral(a)) Bits(a):
  fn countBits(value: a) -> a
  fn leadingZeros(value: a) -> a
  fn trailingZeros(value: a) -> a
  fn bitsUpTo(value: a, count: a) -> a
  fn bitWidth(value: a) -> a

-- Whether exactly one bit is set - `lib/Core/Bits.yana`'s, verbatim. Zero has no bits set and is not
-- one. Here because `Bits`' own fixture asks it, and because it is the shortest thing in the library
-- written over `Integral` and `Eq` together.
pub fn (Integral(a), Eq(a)) isPowerOf2(value: a) -> Bool = value != 0 && (value `and` (value - 1)) == 0

-- The smallest power of two at or above the value - `lib/Core/Bits.yana`'s, verbatim. 1 for 0 and
-- for 1, and a value above the largest power of two the type holds wraps to 0 rather than
-- saturating: the shift is the language's shift, and a saturating answer would be a comparison in
-- the hot path of every growth.
pub fn (Bits(a), Ord(a)) nextPowerOf2(value: a) -> a = if value <= 1 then 1 else 1 `shl` bitWidth(value - 1)
